{
 "scope": "12 real Korean public-sector PDF pages (KDoc-OCRBench-V2 sample) rendered at 200 dpi, OCR via fitwitness.claims.ocr.recognise (kor+eng, MIN_CONF filter 25, OMP threads 1). General Korean public documents, NOT medical forms. n=11 pages with a reference, 1 page unusable for CER.",
 "definitions": {
  "cer": "Levenshtein(ref, ocr)/len(ref), NFKC, all whitespace and invisible chars removed; ref = embedded PDF text layer; OCR = recognise() tokens joined in Tesseract output order (stream order)",
  "cer_geom_order": "same, but both text layer and OCR tokens first re-ordered by geometry (rows top-to-bottom, left-to-right); medium tokens de-rotated by the known degradation angle (geometry only)",
  "digit_recall": "multiset recall of ASCII digits 0-9 (reference digits found in OCR output, order ignored)",
  "number_exact_recall": "share of reference numeric strings (e.g. 1,659,061) that appear exactly in OCR output",
  "medium": "scan.degrade(img, 'medium', scan._seed(filename,'medium','0')): rotate 0.9deg-ish, blur 0.9, noise 10, JPEG 70, contrast 0.85"
 },
 "caveats": [
  "reference text layer is not perfect: p07 misses 2 visible lines, p11 misses logo/some lines (see text_layer_vs_verified_fragments)",
  "text-layer order differs from reading order on some pages (p06) so stream-order CER overstates errors; geometric order has its own noise on tables",
  "rendered digital-born PDFs are cleaner than camera/scanner images; only p08 is a genuine scan",
  "timings measured with 3 OCR processes in parallel on 4 cores"
 ],
